From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 21:49:50 +0000
Subject: net: lantiq: cbm: return CBM buffers on the freeing CPU's own port

The CBM hands a consumed packet segment back to the free-segment manager
by writing its address to a ptr_rtn register, and there is one such
register per CPU dequeue port. Which port a caller picks is therefore not
a detail: it decides who else can be writing the same register at the
same instant.

This driver got that wrong in two connected ways.

The RX bottom half returned every segment it consumed through a fixed
port 2 -- the port host RX is dequeued from -- whichever CPU it happened
to be running on. cbm_fsqm_buf_free() guards that write with
local_irq_save() alone, which excludes this CPU's interrupts and nothing
else, so two of the four load-spreader tasklets running at once on two
CPUs both hammer port 2 and returns are lost. The result is a slow buffer
leak rather than a crash: the free-segment count walks down until the
standard pool is empty and RX starves, with only "FSQM std pool empty" to
show for it.

And DQM CPU port 0 was never configured at all. The port config table
covers ports 2, 1 and 3, which are the ports with a TMU queue behind
them; port 0 has none, so it has no row. The vendor driver brings it up
anyway, with the reason in a comment: "the cbm buffer free API uses DQ
port 0 for freeing". Ours did not, while the TX enqueue-failure rollback
already wrote ptr_rtn on the running CPU's port -- port 0 on any UP boot.
A ptr_rtn write into an unconfigured port neither faults nor logs. It
just drops the segment.

Fix both together, the way the vendor does. Bring up one CPU dequeue port
per online CPU once the config table has been walked, and take the
descriptor count from the port registry instead of a hardcoded 2, so
port 0 -- covered by no row, and not to have dequeue descriptors armed
behind a queue it does not have -- keeps its reset value. Then have the
RX bottom half return segments on smp_processor_id()'s own port, which is
stable across the write because the tasklet runs in softirq context.
Distinct CPUs now write distinct registers and need no mutual exclusion
at all.

Two things fall out of that. Every load-spreader output port has to be
activated in the dequeue bitmask, not just the two that were, because all
four interrupt lines are registered and each can schedule its tasklet;
the host-RX port stays set explicitly on top, since it carries host RX
because of a device-tree redirect rather than because of a CPU count. And
the per-port EQM and DQM interrupts, armed at 0x3f by the port bring-up,
are masked at the end of probe as the vendor masks them -- otherwise,
once TX runs on more than one CPU, ports 1 to 3 start raising into a line
whose handler only ever expected port 0.

While here, bound cbm_dequeue()'s port id from above as the vendor does.
It only rejected negatives, so an out-of-range id addressed past the last
CPU ingress register file.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

